from lib import *
C=10;S='social-science';CH='history-nationalism-gandhian-phase'
def box(x,y,w,h,c,lines,fs=12):
    t=f'<rect x="{x}" y="{y}" width="{w}" height="{h}" rx="8" fill="{c}" stroke="#333"/>'
    for i,l in enumerate(lines): t+=f'<text x="{x+w/2}" y="{y+22+i*(fs+5)}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="{fs}"'+(' font-weight="bold"' if i==0 else '')+f'>{l}</text>'
    return t
def rows(title,data,h=300,rh=36):
    t=f'<text x="280" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">{title}</text>'
    for i,(c,a,b) in enumerate(data):
        t+=f'<rect x="15" y="{36+i*rh}" width="530" height="{rh-5}" rx="6" fill="{c}" stroke="#333"/><text x="23" y="{57+i*rh}" font-size="12" font-weight="bold">{a}</text><text x="130" y="{57+i*rh}" font-size="12">{b}</text>'
    return svg(560,h,t)
d1=rows("Gandhi's early struggles",[('#d6eaf8','1893-1914','South Africa: satyagraha, Phoenix and Tolstoy Farm'),('#d5f5e3','1917','Champaran: tinkathia system abolished'),('#fcf3cf','1918','Ahmedabad mill strike and Kheda satyagraha'),('#fadbd8','1919','Rowlatt Act, 13 April Jallianwala Bagh'),('#ebdef0','1919-20','Khilafat Movement, Ali brothers'),('#fdebd0','1920','Non-Cooperation begins on 1 August')],h=260)
lesson(C,S,CH,'gandhi-early-satyagrahas-rowlatt-and-khilafat',"Gandhi's early satyagrahas, Rowlatt Act and Khilafat","From a railway platform to a nation",
"In 1893 a young lawyer was thrown out of a first class compartment at Pietermaritzburg station because he was Indian. Mohandas Gandhi decided to fight, and the weapon he forged there, satyagraha, would one day shake an empire.",
["Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi was born on 2 October 1869 at Porbandar in Gujarat, where his father Kaba Gandhi was Diwan. @ Gandhi 1869",
 "He sailed to England in 1888, became a barrister in 1891, and went to South Africa in April 1893 to help a Gujarati firm in a law suit. @ South Africa 1893",
 "At Pietermaritzburg station he was physically thrown out of the first class compartment, his first taste of racial discrimination. @ discrimination",
 "In South Africa he developed satyagraha, meaning devotion to the truth or truth-force, with peaceful marches and willing arrest against unjust laws. @ satyagraha",
 "Influenced by Tolstoy, Ruskin and Thoreau, he founded the Phoenix Settlement in 1905 and the Tolstoy Farm in 1910 as training grounds for satyagrahis. @ Phoenix, Tolstoy Farm",
 "The Smuts-Gandhi Agreement finally abolished the poll tax on indentured labourers. @ Smuts-Gandhi Agreement",
 "Gandhi returned to India in 1915 and, on the advice of his political guru Gopal Krishna Gokhale, toured the country to understand the people. @ Gokhale",
 "In Champaran, Bihar, peasants under the tinkathia system had to grow indigo on three-twentieths of their land, and Rajkumar Shukla persuaded Gandhi to visit. @ Champaran",
 "Gandhi defied a police order to leave, and the Lieutenant Governor formed a committee that ended the tinkathia system. @ tinkathia abolished",
 "Success at Champaran, the Ahmedabad mill strike and the Kheda satyagraha, both in 1918, made him a leader of mass struggle. @ Ahmedabad, Kheda 1918",
 "The Rowlatt Act allowed arrest without warrant and detention without trial, so Gandhi called it the Black Act and called a nation-wide satyagraha on 6 April 1919. @ Rowlatt Act",
 "On 13 April 1919, General Dyer blocked the only exit of Jallianwala Bagh and fired for ten minutes, killing 379 by the official count. @ Jallianwala Bagh",
 "Tagore returned his knighthood and Gandhi surrendered his Kaiser-i-Hind medal in protest. @ protest",
 "The Khilafat Movement, led by the Ali brothers, gave Gandhi a chance to unite Hindus and Muslims, and the Khilafat Committee adopted non-cooperation on 9 June 1920. @ Khilafat"],
("In which three satyagrahas did Gandhi first lead Indian peasants and workers?",[("Champaran in 1917 freed peasants from the tinkathia indigo system.","The planters took indigo on three-twentieths of the land."),("Ahmedabad mill workers struck in 1918 and Gandhi guided them.","It was a labour struggle."),("Kheda peasants refused revenue in 1918 after crop failure.","Together these made Gandhi a mass leader.")]),
("Explain the Rowlatt Act and why Indians opposed it.",["Arrest without warrant and detention without trial","Called the Black Act by Gandhi","Nation-wide satyagraha on 6 April 1919"],["Thinking the Rowlatt Act caused the Khilafat Movement"]),
["Gandhi: born 1869, South Africa 1893, returned 1915.","Satyagraha: Champaran 1917, Ahmedabad and Kheda 1918.","Rowlatt Act 1919 and Jallianwala Bagh, 13 April 1919.","Khilafat Movement led to non-cooperation in 1920."],
["n|Year Gandhi returned to India from South Africa|1915|He had spent about twenty years there.",
 "n|Date in April 1919 of the Jallianwala Bagh massacre|13|It happened on Baisakhi day.",
 "m|Gandhi's political guru was|Gopal Krishna Gokhale|Bal Gangadhar Tilak~Not his guru;Lala Lajpat Rai~Not his guru;Dadabhai Naoroji~Not his guru|On his advice Gandhi toured India.",
 "m|The system forcing peasants to grow indigo on three-twentieths of land was|tinkathia|zamindari~A land revenue system;ryotwari~Revenue to individual cultivators;mahalwari~Village revenue|It was practised in Champaran.",
 "t|Gandhi called the Rowlatt Act the Black Act.|True|It denied trial and fair process.",
 "m|Who surrendered the Kaiser-i-Hind medal after Jallianwala Bagh|Gandhi|Tagore~He returned his knighthood;Nehru~No;Jinnah~No|A protest against the brutality.",
 "m|Phoenix Settlement was founded in|1905|1910~Tolstoy Farm;1915~Return to India;1893~Arrival in South Africa|Inspired by Ruskin.",
 "t|The Khilafat Movement was led by the Ali brothers.|True|Maulana Mohamed Ali and Maulana Shaukat Ali.",
 "s|The Smuts-Gandhi Agreement abolished the ____ on indentured labourers.|poll tax|It ended a three pound tax.",
 "s|The first object-lesson in civil disobedience, said Gandhi, was at ____.|Champaran|Bihar 1917."],
svg=(d1,"A timeline table of Gandhi's early struggles from South Africa to 1920","Gandhi's early struggles"))
d2=rows("Non-Cooperation to the Simon boycott",[('#fadbd8','Aug 1920','Non-Cooperation begins, Nagpur session Dec 1920'),('#fcf3cf','5 Feb 1922','Chauri Chaura: 22 policemen die, Gandhi withdraws'),('#d5f5e3','1 Jan 1923','Swaraj Party founded by C.R. Das, Motilal Nehru'),('#d6eaf8','Nov 1927','Simon Commission: all-white, boycotted'),('#ebdef0','1928','Nehru Report: Dominion status, joint electorates'),('#fdebd0','1928','Jinnah Fourteen Points rejected')],h=260)
lesson(C,S,CH,'non-cooperation-swarajists-and-simon-commission',"Non-Cooperation, Swarajists and the Simon Commission","A bonfire of foreign cloth",
"Across India in 1921 great heaps of foreign cloth burned in the streets, lawyers walked out of courts and children left government schools. Then a mob at Chauri Chaura turned violent, and Gandhi stopped everything.",
["The Congress approved non-cooperation at a special Calcutta session in September 1920 and at Nagpur in December 1920, chaired by Salem C. Vijayaraghavachariar. @ Non-Cooperation",
 "It asked people to surrender titles, boycott schools, courts, councils and foreign goods, and spread Swadeshi. @ boycott, Swadeshi",
 "Students left schools, lawyers gave up practice, and the Prince of Wales' visit was boycotted successfully. @ boycott",
 "On 5 February 1922 a procession at Chauri Chaura, near Gorakhpur, turned violent and 22 policemen died when the police station was burnt. @ Chauri Chaura",
 "Gandhi immediately withdrew the movement because he held that only a non-violent struggle could succeed. @ withdrawn",
 "Congress split into pro-changers, who wanted to enter the legislatures, and no-changers like Vallabhbhai Patel and Rajaji, who wanted to continue non-cooperation. @ pro-changers, no-changers",
 "C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru founded the Swaraj Party on 1 January 1923 and won many seats in the Legislative Councils. @ Swaraj Party",
 "Dyarchy under the 1919 Act divided provincial subjects into Reserved and Transferred, and it ended with provincial autonomy in 1935. @ dyarchy",
 "After Chauri Chaura Gandhi's constructive programme promoted khadi, Hindu-Muslim unity and the abolition of untouchability, and the All India Spinners' Association was formed. @ constructive programme",
 "On 8 November 1927 the British appointed the all-white Indian Statutory Commission under Sir John Simon, and everywhere crowds cried Go Back Simon. @ Simon Commission",
 "Lala Lajpat Rai was injured by a police assault in Lahore and died a few days later. @ Lajpat Rai",
 "An all-party conference in 1928 appointed a committee under Motilal Nehru, whose Nehru Report asked for Dominion status, joint electorates and fundamental rights. @ Nehru Report",
 "Jinnah's amendments, called his Fourteen Points, were rejected, and he later began to espouse a separate nation for Muslims. @ Jinnah"],
("Why did Gandhi withdraw the Non-Cooperation Movement?",[("On 5 February 1922 at Chauri Chaura a mob burnt a police station.","22 policemen lost their lives."),("Gandhi believed violence betrayed satyagraha.","The movement rested on non-violence."),("So he called it off, though many leaders were disappointed.","This led to the Swarajist split.")]),
("State the main points of the Nehru Report.",["Dominion status for India","Joint and mixed electorates","Fundamental rights and adult franchise"],["Saying the Simon Commission wrote it"]),
["Non-Cooperation 1920-22, withdrawn after Chauri Chaura.","Swaraj Party formed 1 January 1923.","Simon Commission was all-white, so it was boycotted.","Nehru Report 1928 proposed Dominion status."],
["n|Year of the Chauri Chaura incident|1922|Gandhi then withdrew non-cooperation.",
 "n|Number of policemen who died at Chauri Chaura|22|They were inside the burning police station.",
 "m|The Swaraj Party was founded by|C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru|Vallabhbhai Patel and Rajaji~They were no-changers;Gandhi and Ali~Wrong;Tilak and Gokhale~Earlier leaders|On 1 January 1923.",
 "m|The Simon Commission was boycotted because|it had no Indian member|it was too large~Wrong;it favoured Congress~Wrong;it met in Delhi~Irrelevant|It was an all-white commission.",
 "t|Gandhi presided over the Nagpur Congress session of 1920.|False|It was chaired by Salem C. Vijayaraghavachariar.",
 "t|The Nehru Report recommended Dominion status.|True|It also proposed joint electorates.",
 "m|Who died after a police assault during a Simon protest in Lahore|Lala Lajpat Rai|Bhagat Singh~He lived longer;Rajguru~No;Sukhdev~No|He was injured and died days later.",
 "m|Dyarchy under the Act of 1919 ended in the provinces with|provincial autonomy in 1935|Poona Pact~Wrong;Cripps Mission~Wrong;Simon Commission~Wrong|All provincial subjects went to Indian ministers.",
 "s|The slogan against the Simon Commission was Go ____ Simon.|Back|Shouted at black flag marches.",
 "s|The commonly cited name for Jinnah's 1928 amendments is the ____ Points.|Fourteen|They were rejected."],
svg=(d2,"A timeline table from Non-Cooperation in 1920 to the Nehru Report of 1928","Non-Cooperation to Simon"))
d3=rows("Salt March to Poona Pact",[('#fadbd8','Dec 1929','Lahore: Poorna Swaraj goal, Nehru president'),('#fcf3cf','26 Jan 1930','First Independence Day pledge'),('#d5f5e3','12 Mar-5 Apr 1930','Dandi March, 241 miles in 24 days'),('#d6eaf8','5 Mar 1931','Gandhi-Irwin Pact'),('#ebdef0','16 Aug 1932','Communal Award by Ramsay MacDonald'),('#fdebd0','1932','Poona Pact: 148 reserved seats')],h=260)
lesson(C,S,CH,'civil-disobedience-round-tables-and-poona-pact',"Civil Disobedience, Round Tables and the Poona Pact","A pinch of salt",
"On 6 April 1930 a 61 year old man bent down on the beach at Dandi and picked up a lump of salt. It was against the law, and it set the whole country on fire.",
["At Lahore in December 1929, with Jawaharlal Nehru as President, the Congress declared Poorna Swaraj as its goal. @ Poorna Swaraj",
 "26 January 1930 was declared Independence Day, and a pledge was taken across the country. @ Independence Day",
 "Gandhi sent Lord Irwin a charter of demands including abolition of the salt tax, and the Viceroy did not respond. @ charter of demands",
 "On 12 March 1930 Gandhi set out from Sabarmati with 78 followers and walked 241 miles in 24 days to Dandi. @ Dandi March",
 "At Dandi he broke the salt law by picking up salt on 6 April 1930, and C. Rajaji led a similar march from Tiruchirappalli to Vedaranyam. @ salt law, Vedaranyam",
 "In the North West Frontier Province Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan organised the Khudai Khidmatgar, the Red Shirts. @ Red Shirts",
 "Women, peasants, tribals, students and children took part in the biggest mass movement India had yet seen. @ mass movement",
 "The Gandhi-Irwin Pact of 5 March 1931 released non-violent prisoners and allowed salt making, and Gandhi attended the Second Round Table Conference. @ Gandhi-Irwin Pact",
 "The Round Table Conference failed because Gandhi refused separate electorates for minorities. @ Round Table",
 "Gandhi was arrested on 4 January 1932 when the movement was renewed, and the Congress did not attend the Third Conference. @ renewal",
 "On 16 August 1932 Ramsay MacDonald announced the Communal Award giving separate electorates to minorities and the depressed classes. @ Communal Award",
 "Gandhi fasted unto death from 20 September 1932, and after talks with Ambedkar and M.C. Rajah the Poona Pact was signed. @ Poona Pact",
 "It abandoned separate electorates but raised the reserved seats for the depressed classes from 71 to 148, and 18 percent in the Central Legislature. @ 71 to 148",
 "Gandhi then led a campaign against untouchability, started the Harijan Sevak Sangh, and 8 January 1933 was observed as Temple Entry Day. @ Temple Entry Day"],
("Describe the Salt March and its importance.",[("Gandhi began from Sabarmati on 12 March 1930 with 78 followers.","They walked 241 miles in 24 days."),("At Dandi on 6 April he broke the salt law.","Salt tax touched every household, so it united everyone."),("Similar marches followed, such as Rajaji's to Vedaranyam.","It became a nation-wide movement.")]),
("What were the terms of the Poona Pact?",["Separate electorates abandoned","Reserved seats raised from 71 to 148","18 percent of Central Legislature seats reserved"],["Mixing up the Poona Pact with the Gandhi-Irwin Pact"]),
["Lahore 1929: Poorna Swaraj; 26 January 1930 Independence Day.","Dandi March 12 March to 6 April 1930.","Gandhi-Irwin Pact 5 March 1931; Communal Award 1932.","Poona Pact: joint electorate, seats up from 71 to 148."],
["n|Year of the Dandi March|1930|It broke the salt law.",
 "n|Reserved seats for the depressed classes after the Poona Pact|148|They had been 71.",
 "m|Who led the Salt March in Tamil Nadu|C. Rajaji|Periyar~He was not leading it;Kamaraj~Not that march;Subramania Bharati~Poet|From Tiruchirappalli to Vedaranyam.",
 "m|The Communal Award was announced by|Ramsay MacDonald|Lord Irwin~He signed the Pact;Simon~Commission head;Lord Linlithgow~Later Viceroy|On 16 August 1932.",
 "t|The Poona Pact abolished separate electorates for the depressed classes.|True|Joint electorates with reserved seats replaced them.",
 "t|Gandhi attended the First Round Table Conference.|False|He attended the Second, after the Gandhi-Irwin Pact.",
 "m|The Red Shirts were organised by|Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan|Maulana Azad~No;Rajaji~No;Subhas Bose~No|The Khudai Khidmatgar.",
 "m|Temple Entry Day was observed on|8 January 1933|26 January 1930~Independence Day;5 April 1930~Dandi;20 September 1932~Fast began|Part of the anti-untouchability campaign.",
 "s|The Congress declared ____ Swaraj as its goal at Lahore in 1929.|Poorna|Complete independence.",
 "s|The leader of the depressed classes who negotiated the Poona Pact was Dr. B.R. ____.|Ambedkar|Along with M.C. Rajah."],
svg=(d3,"A timeline table from the Lahore session of 1929 to the Poona Pact of 1932","Salt March to Poona Pact"))
d4=rows("Left movement to Quit India",[('#fadbd8','1920','Communist Party of India founded at Tashkent'),('#fcf3cf','1924-25','HRA formed, Kakori train robbery'),('#d5f5e3','1928-30','Bhagat Singh, Sanders, Assembly bomb, Chittagong raid'),('#d6eaf8','1935-37','Government of India Act, Congress wins 7 of 11'),('#ebdef0','Mar 1942','Cripps Mission fails'),('#fdebd0','8 Aug 1942','Quit India Resolution, Do or Die')],h=260)
lesson(C,S,CH,'revolutionaries-left-movements-and-road-to-quit-india',"Revolutionaries, Left movements and the road to Quit India","Not by the bomb alone",
"After Gandhi suspended non-cooperation, many young Indians lost patience and picked up arms, while others read about the Russian Revolution. Together they pushed the freedom struggle in new directions.",
["The Communist Party of India was founded at Tashkent in October 1920 by M.N. Roy and others, and the Kanpur Conspiracy Case of 1924 tried leaders like Singaravelar. @ Communist Party",
 "In 1924 the Hindustan Republican Army was formed to overthrow colonial rule by armed rebellion, and in 1925 its members looted a government train at Kakori. @ HRA, Kakori",
 "Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev and others renamed it the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association in 1928. @ HSRA",
 "Sanders, the police officer linked to the lathi charge that killed Lajpat Rai, was assassinated, and Bhagat Singh and B.K. Dutt threw a smoke bomb in the Assembly in 1929. @ Bhagat Singh",
 "Surya Sen's Chittagong Armoury Raid of April 1930 declared a provisional revolutionary government. @ Chittagong",
 "In 1934 Jayaprakash Narayan, Acharya Narendra Dev and Minoo Masani formed the Congress Socialist Party. @ Congress Socialist Party",
 "The Government of India Act 1935 offered provincial autonomy with all subjects transferred to Indian ministers, though only about ten percent could vote. @ Act of 1935",
 "In the 1937 elections the Congress won seven of eleven provinces and formed ministries in eight, including Madras. @ Congress ministries 1937",
 "Ministers took a salary of Rs. 500 instead of Rs. 2,000 and repealed repressive acts, and temple entry laws were passed. @ Rs. 500",
 "In 1939 the Congress ministries resigned because the government joined the war without consulting them. @ resignation",
 "In 1940 the August Offer led Gandhi to start individual satyagraha, and Vinoba Bhave offered it first on 17 October 1940. @ individual satyagraha",
 "The Cripps Mission of March 1942 offered Dominion status after the war but failed, and Gandhi called it a post-dated cheque on a crashing bank. @ Cripps Mission",
 "On 8 August 1942 the All India Congress Committee at Bombay passed the Quit India Resolution and Gandhi gave the call to Do or Die. @ Quit India"],
("How did revolutionary and left movements differ from Gandhian methods?",[("Revolutionaries like the HSRA used violence against officials to shake the government.","Bhagat Singh and Surya Sen are examples."),("Left groups like the CPI and the Congress Socialist Party stressed workers and peasants.","They drew on socialism."),("Gandhi relied on mass non-violent action.","Yet all sought freedom.")]),
("Why did the Quit India Movement begin in August 1942?",["Cripps Mission failed","Wartime shortages and price rise","Gandhi's Do or Die call on 8 August"],["Dating the Cripps Mission after Quit India"]),
["Revolutionaries: HRA, HSRA, Kakori, Chittagong.","Left: CPI 1920, Congress Socialist Party 1934.","Act of 1935, Congress ministries, resignation 1939.","Cripps failed, Quit India resolution on 8 August 1942."],
["n|Year the Congress Socialist Party was formed|1934|By Jayaprakash Narayan and others.",
 "n|Number of provinces where Congress formed ministries in 1937 (of eleven)|8|It won seven outright.",
 "m|The Chittagong Armoury Raid was led by|Surya Sen|Bhagat Singh~Punjab revolutionary;Ashfaqulla Khan~Kakori;Rajguru~HSRA|In April 1930.",
 "m|Who offered the first individual satyagraha|Vinoba Bhave|Jawaharlal Nehru~Not first;Rajaji~Not first;Patel~Not first|On 17 October 1940.",
 "t|The Cripps Mission succeeded in winning Congress support.|False|Both Congress and the League rejected it.",
 "t|Congress ministries resigned in 1939 over the war.|True|India was dragged in without consultation.",
 "m|The Hindustan Socialist Republican Association was formed in|1928|1920~CPI founded;1934~Congress Socialist Party;1942~Quit India|By Bhagat Singh and comrades.",
 "m|Gandhi called the Cripps proposals|a post-dated cheque on a crashing bank|a golden bridge~Wrong;a fair offer~Wrong;a final settlement~Wrong|Because power was not transferred.",
 "s|The Quit India resolution was passed at ____ in August 1942.|Bombay|By the All India Congress Committee.",
 "s|Gandhi's slogan in 1942 was Do or ____.|Die|He gave this call in Bombay."],
svg=(d4,"A timeline table from the Communist Party in 1920 to the Quit India resolution in 1942","Left movement to Quit India"))
